## 1. Stripe launched a private B2B payment network

On **October 27**, Stripe introduced **Stripe Pay**, allowing one Stripe business to find another by its Stripe Profile handle and send an instant, fee-free transfer from either the Stripe Dashboard or command-line interface. The initial announcement describes the service as available now.

**How the flow works:** Instead of collecting a supplier’s bank account and routing information, the sender selects its public Stripe handle. Stripe identifies the recipient and processes the transfer between the businesses.

Stripe has not said that the transfer itself travels over RTP, FedNow or another external bank rail. The interpretation that this is an internal balance transfer remains an inference. Funding Stripe and withdrawing money to a bank are separate steps.

**Why it matters:** Stripe already knows its merchants’ identities, bank accounts and payment histories. That lets it create a directory-based network without asking businesses to exchange banking credentials. It resembles a business version of Venmo, accessible through business software and developer tools.

**Retail-banking implication:** Banks may increasingly provide the entry and exit accounts while a payments platform controls the directory, transaction interface and payment data. A business customer could perceive a Stripe-to-Stripe transfer as immediate even if moving the resulting balance into its checking account takes longer. Bankers should distinguish the platform balance from immediately available bank deposits.

**Status:** Available now, according to Stripe’s announcement.

**Sources:** [Stripe announcement on LinkedIn](https://www.linkedin.com/company/stripe/) · [October 27 coverage from PYMNTS](https://www.pymnts.com/news/b2b-payments/2026/stripe-pay-targets-instant-b2b-payments/)

## 2. Malaysian banking apps can now pay foreign QR merchants

On **October 28**, Malaysia’s PayNet activated outbound DuitNow QR connectivity through Alipay+. Customers of Maybank, CIMB and Hong Leong Bank can use their existing mobile-banking apps at participating merchants in China, Hong Kong, South Korea, the Philippines and Laos. Bank Islam is expected to join later.

**How the flow works:**

1. The customer opens DuitNow QR inside the bank’s app.
2. The customer scans a foreign merchant’s Alipay+ QR code.
3. PayNet and Alipay+ connect the Malaysian payment service with the merchant’s payment provider.
4. The customer reviews and confirms the transaction in the familiar banking app.

The customer does not need an Alipay+ account or another wallet. The bank app has effectively become an internationally accepted payment wallet. The announcement does not specify the complete interbank settlement arrangements.

**Why it matters:** This is a live example of interoperability expanding a domestic payment scheme without replacing local bank apps or requiring every bank to integrate separately with every foreign QR system.

**Retail-banking implication:** Banks can retain the customer interface even when an external gateway supplies international acceptance. That preserves opportunities for transaction alerts, travel controls, dispute support and foreign-exchange services. It also complicates servicing: the customer sees the bank’s app, while PayNet, Alipay+ and a foreign payment provider may all participate in the payment.

**Status:** Live through three launch banks at participating merchants in five destinations.

**Source:** [PayNet — Overseas DuitNow QR payments through Alipay+](https://paynet.my/about-us/media-centre/press-release/paynet-enables-malaysian-mobile-banking-app-users-to-make-overseas-duitnow-qr-payments-via-alipayplus.html)

## 3. Visa passkeys reached live merchant checkout

On **October 29**, Checkout.com said it was live with **Visa Payment Passkey**. The phased rollout begins with selected merchants in the UK and Europe; it is not yet broadly available to every Checkout.com merchant.

**How the technology works:** During an online Visa purchase, the customer verifies the payment with the fingerprint, face scan or PIN used to unlock the device. A FIDO cryptographic key confirms possession of the registered device. The private key remains on the device, so there is no SMS code for a criminal to intercept or persuade the customer to disclose.

If passkey authentication is unavailable, the checkout falls back to existing verification methods.

This changes the **cardholder-authentication step**. The payment still uses the Visa card network, and the issuer separately decides whether to authorize it.

**Why it matters:** Merchants can add stronger authentication through Checkout.com’s existing integration rather than rebuilding checkout. The potential benefit is fewer abandoned purchases alongside reduced exposure to stolen one-time codes.

**Retail-banking implication:** Passkeys should reduce reliance on one-time codes, along with associated phishing and SIM-swap risks. Issuers will still make authorization decisions using balance, fraud and account information. Servicing systems should show whether a purchase passed device-based authentication separately from whether the issuer approved the transaction.

**Status:** Live, limited rollout beginning with selected UK and European merchants.

